Distributed Communication — Message Queues
Think of a message queue as a mechanism that changes this:
Synchronous

Producer ────────request────────> Consumer
          <──────response────────
into this:
Asynchronous

Producer ──message──> Broker/Queue ──message──> Consumer
                         │
                         ├── persists message
                         ├── tracks delivery
                         └── handles failures
The important insight is:
A message queue is not primarily about moving messages. It is about decoupling the failure, timing, and execution characteristics of distributed components.
We'll study these concepts in this order:
1.	Why asynchronous communication exists
2.	Queue internals
3.	Producer → Broker → Consumer
4.	Acknowledgement, delivery & redelivery
5.	Ordering
6.	Durability
7.	Failure scenarios
8.	Design exercise
________________________________________
1. Why asynchronous communication exists
Let's start without queues.
Imagine an e-commerce system:
Client
  |
  v
Order Service
  |
  +----> Payment Service
  |
  +----> Inventory Service
  |
  +----> Notification Service
  |
  +----> Analytics Service
Suppose the user places an order.
A naive implementation might do:
placeOrder() {
    saveOrder();

    paymentService.charge();

    inventoryService.reserve();

    notificationService.sendEmail();

    analyticsService.record();

    return SUCCESS;
}
This creates a very important dependency:
Order Service
     |
     +---- Payment
     |
     +---- Inventory
     |
     +---- Notification
     |
     +---- Analytics
The order service's latency and availability now depend on every downstream service.
________________________________________
1.1 Latency coupling
Suppose:
Payment       = 100 ms
Inventory     = 50 ms
Notification  = 200 ms
Analytics     = 500 ms
Sequentially:
Total ≈ 850 ms
Even if analytics isn't important to completing the order.
You could parallelize:
             ┌── Payment ─────── 100ms
             |
Order ───────┼── Inventory ───── 50ms
             |
             ├── Notification ── 200ms
             |
             └── Analytics ───── 500ms
but now you're still coupled to whether those services are available.
________________________________________
2. Availability coupling
Suppose Notification Service goes down.
With synchronous communication:
Order Service
      |
      +---- Notification Service ❌
You have to decide:
Should order creation fail?
But perhaps notification isn't critical.
A queue changes the relationship:
Order Service
      |
      v
Notification Queue
      |
      v
Notification Worker
Now:
Order Service → Queue
can succeed even when:
Notification Worker = DOWN
The queue acts as a buffer.
________________________________________
3. Temporal decoupling
This is one of the most important concepts.
Suppose the producer operates at:
10,000 messages/sec
but the consumer can process only:
2,000 messages/sec
Without a queue:
Producer ─────────> Consumer
10K/sec              2K/sec
The consumer becomes a bottleneck.
With a queue:
Producer
  |
  | 10K/sec
  v
+----------------+
| Queue          |
|                |
| ████████████   |
| ████████████   |
+----------------+
       |
       | 2K/sec
       v
    Consumer
The queue absorbs the temporary difference.
This is called buffering.
________________________________________
4. The queue doesn't eliminate overload
This is a subtle but extremely important distributed-systems concept.
Suppose:
Producer = 10,000 msg/sec
Consumer = 2,000 msg/sec
Then queue growth is:
10,000 - 2,000
= 8,000 messages/sec
Eventually:
Queue capacity → exhausted
So queues provide:
Temporal decoupling, not infinite capacity.
This is why production systems monitor:
Queue depth
Consumer throughput
Producer throughput
Message age
Consumer lag
________________________________________
5. Why asynchronous communication exists
We can now identify the major reasons.
Synchronous communication
A ─────request────> B
A <────response──── B
A waits for B.
Asynchronous communication
A ───message───> Queue
                  |
                  | later
                  v
                  B
A doesn't need B to be ready immediately.
This gives us:
•	Temporal decoupling
•	Failure isolation
•	Load smoothing
•	Independent scaling
•	Work distribution
•	Retry capability
•	Buffering
•	Reduced producer latency
•	Loose coupling between services
________________________________________
6. But asynchronous communication introduces complexity
This is where distributed systems gets interesting.
You haven't eliminated the problem.
You've moved it.
Instead of:
Request succeeded / failed
you now have:
Message accepted
       ↓
Message persisted?
       ↓
Message delivered?
       ↓
Consumer processed?
       ↓
Consumer acknowledged?
       ↓
Consumer crashed?
       ↓
Message redelivered?
Therefore asynchronous systems introduce questions such as:
Did my message actually get stored?
Did the consumer receive it?
Did the consumer process it?
Did the consumer acknowledge it?
What happens if the consumer crashes after processing but before acknowledgement?
Those questions lead directly to delivery semantics.
We'll get there.
________________________________________
7. Queue internals
Let's now look underneath the abstraction.
A simplified queue:
+------------------------------------------------+
|                 Message Broker                 |
|                                                |
|  Queue                                         |
|                                                |
|  [M1] [M2] [M3] [M4] [M5] [M6] [M7]           |
|   ^                                             |
|   |                                             |
|  head                                           |
|                                                |
|                         tail ---------------->  |
+------------------------------------------------+
Conceptually we have:
head
  ↓
[M1][M2][M3][M4][M5]
                   ↑
                  tail
Producer:
append(M6)
Consumer:
read(M1)
But real distributed queues are considerably more complicated.
