9. Two important queue implementation models
At system-design level, distinguish these concepts.
Traditional queue
Conceptually:
enqueue()
   ↓
[M1][M2][M3][M4]
 ^
 |
dequeue()
Once consumed successfully, the message may be removed.
This is close to the mental model of:
FIFO queue
________________________________________
Log-based messaging
A system such as Kafka is better understood as a distributed append-only log.
Conceptually:
Offset:

0    1    2    3    4    5
|    |    |    |    |    |
v    v    v    v    v    v

[M0] [M1] [M2] [M3] [M4] [M5]
A consumer has a position:
Consumer offset = 3
Meaning:
processed/read through offset 2
next = offset 3
The message isn't necessarily deleted when consumed.
This distinction is fundamental.
________________________________________
10. Queue vs log
Traditional queue:
Producer
   |
   v
[M1][M2][M3][M4]
 ^
 |
Consumer
After successful consumption:
[M2][M3][M4]
Log:
[M1][M2][M3][M4][M5]
              ^
              |
         Consumer offset
The data can remain.
Multiple consumers can independently maintain positions:
Log:

[M0][M1][M2][M3][M4][M5][M6][M7]

Consumer A
             ^
            offset 3

Consumer B
                   ^
                  offset 5
That's one reason log-based systems are powerful for event streaming.
________________________________________
11. Producer → Broker → Consumer
Now let's follow one message.
Suppose:
{
  "orderId": 123,
  "amount": 500
}
The producer sends it.
Producer
   |
   | MESSAGE
   v
Broker
   |
   | MESSAGE
   v
Consumer
But there are actually several stages.
________________________________________
Producer side
Application
    |
    v
Producer Client
    |
    v
Network
    |
    v
Broker
The producer client may:
•	serialize the message
•	add metadata
•	choose destination
•	choose partition
•	batch messages
•	compress messages
•	retry failed requests
________________________________________
12. Broker responsibilities
The broker generally handles things such as:
Receive message
      ↓
Validate
      ↓
Determine queue/partition
      ↓
Persist
      ↓
Replicate
      ↓
Make available to consumers
Depending on the system, not every step has the same guarantee.
This is why asking:
"Did the broker receive my message?"
is insufficient.
We need to ask:
At what point does the broker tell the producer "success"?
That's where acknowledgements and durability intersect.
________________________________________
13. Consumer side
Consumer:
Broker
   |
   | fetch/push
   v
Consumer Client
   |
   v
Application Handler
For example:
Message message = consumer.receive();

process(message);

consumer.ack(message);
The ordering of those operations is incredibly important.
________________________________________
14. Acknowledgement
Suppose:
Broker
   |
   | M1
   v
Consumer
Consumer processes:
process(M1)
Then tells broker:
ACK(M1)
Meaning approximately:
"I have successfully handled this message; you don't need to deliver it again."
The exact semantics vary between messaging systems.
________________________________________
15. The classic crash scenario
Consider:
Broker
   |
   | M1
   v
Consumer
Consumer executes:
process(M1);
Processing succeeds.
But before:
ack(M1);
the process crashes.
Consumer
   |
   X
 CRASH
Broker thinks:
M1 = not acknowledged
Therefore:
M1 → redelivery
When the consumer restarts:
Broker
   |
   | M1 again
   v
Consumer
Now M1 may be processed twice.
This is one of the most important scenarios in distributed messaging.
________________________________________
16. Therefore: "exactly once" is difficult
People often casually say:
"We'll make the queue exactly once."
But there are multiple operations:
Read message
     ↓
Process business logic
     ↓
Write database
     ↓
Acknowledge message
Imagine:
1. Receive M1
2. Update DB
3. DB commit succeeds
4. Consumer crashes
5. ACK never happens
After restart:
M1 gets delivered again
Then:
UPDATE database
happens again.
Therefore, the consumer should often be idempotent.
For example:
messageId = 12345
Maintain:
ProcessedMessages
-----------------
12345
Then:
if (alreadyProcessed(message.id())) {
    return;
}

process(message);

markProcessed(message.id());
But even this has a transaction-ordering problem, which we'll explore later.
________________________________________
17. Delivery semantics
Now we can define three commonly discussed semantics.
At-most-once
Message
   ↓
Deliver
   ↓
Consider done
If processing fails:
Message lost
Guarantee:
Message is delivered zero or one times.
No duplicates, but possible loss.
________________________________________
At-least-once
Broker keeps the message until acknowledgement.
Deliver
   ↓
Process
   ↓
ACK
If ACK doesn't happen:
REDO / REDELIVER
Guarantee:
Message will be delivered one or more times, assuming eventual recovery.
Potential issue:
duplicates
This is extremely common in distributed systems.
________________________________________
Exactly-once
Conceptually:
Message effect occurs exactly once
This is much harder.
Notice the word effect.
Exactly-once delivery is not necessarily the same thing as exactly-once business processing.
For example:
Message → Consumer → Database
If database transaction and message acknowledgement aren't atomically coordinated, failures create ambiguity.
________________________________________
18. Ordering
Now let's tackle ordering.
Suppose producer generates:
M1
M2
M3
M4
You might expect:
Consumer receives:

M1
M2
M3
M4
But distributed systems make this nontrivial.
________________________________________
Multiple consumers
Suppose:
             ┌── Consumer A
Queue ───────┤
             └── Consumer B
Messages:
M1 M2 M3 M4
Could be processed:
Consumer A → M1, M3
Consumer B → M2, M4
Actual completion order could become:
M2
M1
M4
M3
Even if the queue itself preserves enqueue order.
So we must distinguish:
message ordering
from
processing/completion ordering
________________________________________
19. Partitioning solves some ordering problems
Suppose we have orders:
Order A
Order B
Order C
We can partition based on:
orderId
For example:
hash(orderId) % 3
Result:
Partition 0
[A1][A2][A3]

Partition 1
[B1][B2]

Partition 2
[C1][C2][C3]
Within one partition:
A1 → A2 → A3
can preserve ordering.
Across partitions:
A1
B1
C1
A2
C2
there may be no global ordering.
This leads to a very important design principle:
Ordering is usually purchased by reducing concurrency.
Global ordering + massive parallelism is fundamentally difficult.
________________________________________
20. Durability
Now suppose:
Producer
   |
   v
Broker
Producer sends:
M1
Broker responds:
SUCCESS
Then broker crashes.
Question:
Is M1 still there?
Depends on what "SUCCESS" meant.
________________________________________
In-memory queue
RAM:

[M1][M2][M3]
Broker crashes:
RAM → lost
Messages disappear.
________________________________________
Disk persistence
RAM
 ↓
Disk
Broker can recover messages from disk.
But even disk persistence isn't enough to automatically guarantee survival against every failure.
Suppose:
Broker A
   |
   | replication
   v
Broker B
If A crashes before replication:
A = lost
B = doesn't have M1
M1 may still be lost.
So durable distributed messaging often involves:
Persistence
+
Replication
+
Acknowledgement policy
________________________________________
21. The producer acknowledgement problem
Imagine:
Producer → Broker A
Broker A receives M1.
Three possible moments for success:
ACK immediately
Producer
   |
   | M1
   v
Broker A
   |
   +--> ACK
Fast, but perhaps not durable.
ACK after disk write
M1
 ↓
Disk
 ↓
ACK
Better durability.
ACK after replication
             ┌── Broker A
Producer ────┤
             └── Broker B
                    |
                   ACK
Potentially stronger durability.
But:
More guarantees
     ↓
More latency
This is a recurring distributed-system trade-off.
________________________________________
22. Failure matrix
Let's build the mental model you should use in system-design interviews.
Failure	Possible consequence
Producer crashes before send	Message never exists
Network fails during send	Producer doesn't know whether broker received it
Broker crashes before persistence	Message may be lost
Broker crashes after persistence	Message can recover
Consumer crashes before processing	Redelivery
Consumer crashes after processing, before ACK	Duplicate processing
Consumer is slow	Queue/lag grows
Consumer permanently fails	Messages accumulate
Network partition	Delivery/ack uncertainty
Broker replica fails	Depends on replication policy
That one table captures much of the reason messaging systems are difficult.
________________________________________
23. The most important ambiguity
Consider:
Producer ── M1 ──> Broker
Producer sends M1.
Then network breaks:
Producer       X       Broker
Producer asks:
Did broker receive M1?
It may be impossible for the producer to know.
This is a classic distributed-systems problem.
Therefore producer may retry:
M1
 ↓
retry
 ↓
M1
Now the broker might contain:
M1
M1
Hence producer retries can create duplicates.
This is another reason idempotency matters.
