Lesson 6 — Backpressure, Retries, Poison Messages & Dead-Letter Queues
This lesson is extremely important for production system design.
So far, we've mostly asked:
"How do we move messages reliably?"
Now we ask:
"What happens when the consumer cannot keep up?"
This is where otherwise-correct distributed systems start collapsing.
________________________________________
1. The fundamental problem: producer faster than consumer
Imagine:
Producer
  |
  | 100,000 msg/sec
  v
Broker
  |
  | 20,000 msg/sec
  v
Consumer
Incoming:
100,000/sec
Processing:
20,000/sec
Therefore backlog grows:
100,000 - 20,000
= 80,000 messages/sec
After 1 minute:
80,000 × 60
= 4.8 million messages
Your queue is now carrying millions of messages.
This is consumer lag.
________________________________________
2. Queue is a shock absorber
This is one of the most useful ways to think about a message broker.
Without a queue:
Producer ─────→ Consumer
If consumer slows down:
Producer ──X──→ Consumer
The producer must slow down or requests fail.
With a queue:
Producer
   |
   v
+--------+
| Queue  |
+--------+
   |
   v
Consumer
Temporary traffic spikes can be absorbed:
Traffic spike
     ↓
Queue grows
     ↓
Consumer catches up later
But:
A queue is not magic storage for infinite backlog.
If the consumer is permanently slower, the queue eventually fills.
________________________________________
3. Backpressure
Backpressure means:
The downstream system communicates its inability to accept more work, causing upstream systems to slow down or limit work.
Think of water:
Pipe A
  ↓
████████████
     ↓
  small pipe
     ↓
   Drain
If water arrives faster than the drain can handle:
water level ↑
Eventually:
overflow
Messaging systems have the same problem.
________________________________________
4. Why simply increasing the queue isn't enough
Suppose:
Producer = 100K/sec
Consumer = 20K/sec
You increase queue capacity from:
10 million
to:
1 billion
You haven't solved the throughput mismatch.
You've only delayed the failure.
Eventually:
1 billion messages
will also be consumed too slowly.
So the first question should be:
Why is the consumer slower?
Possibilities:
•	insufficient consumer instances
•	slow database
•	slow downstream API
•	CPU bottleneck
•	network bottleneck
•	external rate limit
•	lock contention
•	garbage collection
•	inefficient message processing
________________________________________
5. Scaling consumers
Suppose:
1 consumer = 5,000 msg/sec
and:
incoming = 100,000 msg/sec
Then roughly:
100,000 / 5,000 = 20 consumers
So:
               Broker
                  |
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
      C1         C2         C3
      ...
      C20
can theoretically handle:
20 × 5,000
= 100,000 msg/sec
But don't design exactly at the limit.
You need headroom.
Maybe:
capacity = 150K/sec
expected = 100K/sec
So a temporary spike doesn't immediately create a growing backlog.
________________________________________
6. But partitions limit scaling
Remember Lesson 4.
Suppose:
Partitions = 10
but you deploy:
Consumers = 30
Only roughly 10 consumers can actively own partitions within that consumer group.
So:
Consumer scaling
requires sufficient:
Partition parallelism
This is why partition count is also a throughput-design decision.
________________________________________
7. The more dangerous problem: downstream dependency
Now consider:
Broker
   |
   v
Notification Service
   |
   v
External SMS Provider
Your consumer can process:
50,000 msg/sec
But the SMS provider only allows:
5,000/sec
Then increasing consumers is actually harmful.
You might create:
20 consumers
all hammering the provider.
Result:
SMS Provider
     ↓
Rate limit
     ↓
429 / timeout
     ↓
Retries
Now the system becomes much worse.
________________________________________
8. Retry storm
Suppose:
100,000 requests
are sent.
External service fails.
Every consumer retries immediately:
100K original
+
100K retries
+
100K retry retries
...
You get:
Failure
  ↓
Retry
  ↓
More load
  ↓
More failures
  ↓
More retry
  ↓
More load
This is a retry storm.
The recovery mechanism itself becomes the cause of the outage.
________________________________________
9. Never retry immediately by default
Bad:
request fails
    ↓
retry immediately
    ↓
fails
    ↓
retry immediately
    ↓
fails
Better:
failure
   ↓
wait
   ↓
retry
   ↓
wait longer
   ↓
retry
This is exponential backoff.
________________________________________
10. Exponential backoff
For example:
Retry 1 → 1 sec
Retry 2 → 2 sec
Retry 3 → 4 sec
Retry 4 → 8 sec
Retry 5 → 16 sec
Formula:
delay = initialDelay × 2^retryNumber
Usually you also put a maximum:
maxDelay = 60 sec
So it becomes:
1
2
4
8
16
32
60
60
60
rather than growing forever.
________________________________________
11. Why jitter matters
Imagine 100,000 consumers all experience failure at:
12:00:00
Without jitter:
Retry 1 → 12:00:01
Retry 2 → 12:00:03
Retry 3 → 12:00:07
Everyone retries at exactly the same time.
You get synchronized traffic spikes.
Instead:
delay = exponentialBackoff + randomJitter
Example:
Consumer 1 → 1.2 sec
Consumer 2 → 1.7 sec
Consumer 3 → 0.9 sec
Consumer 4 → 1.4 sec
The retry traffic spreads out.
This is why jitter is a very important production detail.
________________________________________
12. Retryable vs non-retryable errors
This distinction is crucial.
Suppose payment fails because:
HTTP 503
Probably retryable.
But:
HTTP 400
Invalid card details
Retrying won't fix it.
So classify errors:
                    Error
                      |
            ┌─────────┴─────────┐
            ↓                   ↓
        Retryable          Non-retryable
            |                   |
        retry later          DLQ / reject
Examples of potentially retryable errors:
•	timeout
•	temporary network failure
•	503
•	temporary database unavailability
•	rate limiting
Potentially non-retryable:
•	malformed message
•	invalid schema
•	invalid business data
•	missing required field
•	authentication/configuration error that won't change
The exact classification depends on the downstream system.
________________________________________
13. Poison messages
Now imagine:
M1
M2
M3
M4
M3 is malformed.
Consumer does:
M1 ✓
M2 ✓
M3 ❌
So it retries:
M3
Again:
M3 ❌
Again:
M3 ❌
If your system keeps retrying forever:
M3 → M3 → M3 → M3 → ...
This is a poison message.
It can consume resources indefinitely.
________________________________________
14. Dead-letter queue
The standard solution is a DLQ.
Main Queue
     |
     v
 Consumer
     |
     | failure
     v
 Retry
     |
     | too many failures
     v
 Dead Letter Queue
Example:
Main Queue
    |
    v
M3
    |
  retry 1
    |
  retry 2
    |
  retry 3
    |
    v
  DLQ
Now normal traffic can continue.
________________________________________
15. What goes into a DLQ?
Usually don't put only the original message.
You want diagnostic metadata too.
Conceptually:
{
  "originalMessage": "...",
  "messageId": "12345",
  "failureReason": "InvalidSchema",
  "retryCount": 5,
  "firstFailedAt": "...",
  "lastFailedAt": "...",
  "consumer": "payment-worker",
  "errorType": "..."
}
This helps operators understand what happened.
________________________________________
16. DLQ isn't "delete the problem"
A common beginner mistake:
"Put it in DLQ and forget it."
No.
A DLQ is essentially:
A holding area for messages that normal processing could not successfully handle.
You need an operational process:
DLQ
 |
 +→ inspect
 |
 +→ identify cause
 |
 +→ fix bug/data
 |
 +→ replay
For example:
Bad deployment
     ↓
10,000 messages → DLQ
     ↓
Rollback/fix
     ↓
Replay DLQ
________________________________________
17. Retry queue vs DLQ
These are different.
Retry queue
Temporary:
Main
 ↓
Retry
 ↓
Main
DLQ
Terminal-ish holding area:
Main
 ↓
Retries exhausted
 ↓
DLQ
So:
Retry queue = "try again later"

DLQ = "normal processing failed; investigate"
________________________________________
18. Delayed retry architecture
A common architecture:
                  ┌──────────────┐
                  │ Main Topic   │
                  └──────┬───────┘
                         ↓
                      Consumer
                         |
                ┌────────┴────────┐
                ↓                 ↓
              Success           Failure
                |                 |
                ↓                 ↓
               ACK          Retry Queue
                                  |
                              wait 10 sec
                                  |
                                  ↓
                             Main Topic
Then after repeated failures:
Retry Queue
    |
    | max retries
    v
   DLQ
________________________________________
19. Don't block the main queue unnecessarily
Suppose:
M1 → success
M2 → poison
M3 → success
M4 → success
If you block the entire queue waiting for M2:
M1 ✓
M2 ❌
M3 blocked
M4 blocked
throughput collapses.
But if the system can safely move M2 aside:
M1 ✓
M2 → retry/DLQ
M3 ✓
M4 ✓
you preserve throughput.
However, remember our ordering lesson:
You cannot blindly skip a failed message if subsequent messages depend on it.
For example:
Account:
Deposit
Withdraw
CloseAccount
If Deposit fails permanently, processing Withdraw might be invalid.
So DLQ behavior must respect business ordering semantics.
________________________________________
20. Consumer lag
Now let's quantify backlog.
Suppose:
Incoming = 50K/sec
Processing = 40K/sec
Lag grows:
10K/sec
After 10 minutes:
10,000 × 600
= 6 million messages
That's a lot.
But backlog size alone isn't the most useful metric.
We also care about:
How old is the oldest unprocessed message?
Suppose:
oldest message age = 20 minutes
That tells us users may be experiencing 20-minute delays.
So useful metrics include:
consumer lag
messages behind
oldest message age
processing latency
retry rate
DLQ rate
________________________________________
21. Queueing theory intuition
Let's define:
λ = arrival rate
μ = service rate
If:
λ < μ
the system can eventually keep up.
If:
λ > μ
backlog grows continuously.
For example:
λ = 80K/sec
μ = 100K/sec
Healthy.
But:
λ = 120K/sec
μ = 100K/sec
backlog grows:
20K/sec
If the spike is temporary:
λ eventually drops
consumer can catch up.
If it's permanent:
system is under-capacity
You must scale or reduce incoming work.
________________________________________
22. Backpressure strategies
There isn't one universal technique.
Strategy 1 — Producer throttling
Tell producers:
Slow down.
Example:
100K/sec → 50K/sec
Useful when producers can tolerate delayed work.
________________________________________
Strategy 2 — Rate limiting
Consumer limits downstream calls:
Max 5K requests/sec
Useful for external APIs.
________________________________________
Strategy 3 — Buffering
Allow queue backlog temporarily:
Traffic spike
   ↓
Queue grows
   ↓
Traffic normalizes
   ↓
Queue drains
Good for short bursts.
________________________________________
Strategy 4 — Load shedding
Discard work that isn't valuable enough.
For example:
real-time analytics event
might be dropped under extreme overload.
But:
payment event
should not be dropped.
So load shedding depends on business priority.
________________________________________
Strategy 5 — Priority queues
Separate:
High priority
Normal priority
Low priority
For example:
Payment confirmation
      ↓
High priority

Marketing analytics
      ↓
Low priority
Under overload:
High priority continues
Low priority slows/stops
________________________________________
23. Backpressure across multiple services
Now consider:
Order Service
      |
      v
Order Queue
      |
      v
Payment Service
      |
      v
Payment Gateway
Suppose Payment Gateway slows down.
Then:
Payment Service
      ↓
slows
      ↓
Order Queue
      ↓
backlog increases
      ↓
Order Service
If Order Service keeps producing indefinitely:
entire system
can eventually become overloaded.
This is backpressure propagation.
________________________________________
24. The failure amplification chain
This is one of the most important patterns to understand.
Downstream slowdown
       ↓
Consumer latency ↑
       ↓
Consumer throughput ↓
       ↓
Queue lag ↑
       ↓
Memory/storage pressure ↑
       ↓
Consumer/broker instability
       ↓
More failures
       ↓
Retries ↑
       ↓
Traffic ↑
       ↓
Downstream becomes even worse
This is a positive feedback loop.
A well-designed distributed system tries to break this loop.
________________________________________
25. Circuit breaker + queue
Suppose downstream service is clearly unhealthy.
Instead of:
Consumer
  |
  +→ call downstream
  +→ call downstream
  +→ call downstream
  +→ call downstream
use a circuit breaker:
Consumer
   |
   v
Circuit Breaker
   |
   X
Downstream
After enough failures:
Circuit = OPEN
The consumer stops hammering the dependency.
Then:
wait
 ↓
half-open
 ↓
test request
 ↓
if healthy → close
Combined with queues:
Broker
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
Circuit Breaker
  X
Downstream
Messages remain buffered/retried according to your policy instead of generating uncontrolled traffic.
________________________________________
26. A very important design distinction
Consider:
Consumer receives message
       ↓
calls external API
       ↓
API succeeds
       ↓
consumer crashes before ACK
The broker redelivers.
Now:
Consumer
   ↓
calls API again
So even a successful downstream operation can result in a duplicate.
This is why retries and redelivery inevitably bring us back to:
Idempotency
For example:
paymentId = P123
Consumer sends:
processPayment(P123)
The payment service maintains:
P123 → already processed
Second attempt becomes:
P123 already completed
→ don't charge again
This is one of the most important patterns in distributed systems.
