23. Why Kafka uses sequential writes
A central performance idea:
Random write:
disk → seek → write
disk → seek → write
disk → seek → write
versus:
Sequential:
write → write → write → write
Kafka's append-only log architecture is designed to make writes largely sequential.
That gives Kafka an important performance advantage.
Modern hardware changes the exact performance characteristics, especially with SSDs, but the append-log architecture remains fundamental.
________________________________________
24. OS page cache
Kafka historically relies heavily on the operating system's page cache.
Conceptually:
Producer
   |
   v
Kafka process
   |
   v
OS page cache
   |
   v
Disk
Consumers can also benefit from cached data:
Disk
  ↓
OS page cache
  ↓
Kafka
  ↓
Consumer
This means Kafka doesn't need to implement every layer of caching itself.
The OS is doing useful work.
________________________________________
25. Zero-copy
Another important Kafka performance concept is zero-copy transfer.
Conceptually, instead of repeatedly copying bytes:
Disk
 ↓
Kafka buffer
 ↓
Application buffer
 ↓
Socket buffer
 ↓
Consumer
the operating system can use mechanisms such as:
sendfile
to transfer file data more efficiently.
Conceptually:
Disk
  |
  | efficient kernel-level transfer
  v
Network Socket
  |
  v
Consumer
This reduces unnecessary user-space copying.
You don't need to memorize the syscall yet.
Understand the principle:
Kafka's log-oriented architecture allows the broker to move already-persisted bytes efficiently rather than treating every message as an object that must be fully materialized and copied through application memory.
