Lesson 2 — Kafka Topic + Partition Internals
This lesson is where Kafka starts becoming interesting from a systems-engineering perspective.
The key question is:
What actually exists on disk when I create a Kafka topic with 3 partitions?
We’ll go from logical topic → partition → log segment → record batch → index → retention.
________________________________________
1. Topic is a logical abstraction
Suppose you create:
orders
with 3 partitions:
orders
 ├── P0
 ├── P1
 └── P2
The topic itself doesn't contain one giant log.
Instead, Kafka has three independent ordered logs.
P0:  0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4
P1:  0 → 1 → 2 → 3
P2:  0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
This immediately gives us two properties:
Ordering
Kafka can guarantee:
P0:
100 → 101 → 102 → 103
But cannot guarantee:
P0 offset 100
before
P1 offset 50
because those are independent logs.
Parallelism
Different consumers can process:
P0 → Consumer A
P1 → Consumer B
P2 → Consumer C
So:
Partition is Kafka's fundamental unit of ordering and parallelism.
Keep that sentence.
________________________________________
2. What is physically stored?
Imagine broker 1 owns partition 0.
Conceptually, its storage might look like:
Kafka data directory

orders-0/
orders-1/
payments-0/
...
For:
orders-0
Kafka doesn't necessarily keep everything in one enormous file.
It divides the partition into log segments.
orders-0/

Segment 1
Segment 2
Segment 3
Segment 4
Each segment contains a portion of the partition's log.
________________________________________
3. Why segments?
Imagine a partition has:
10 billion records
If Kafka stored that in one giant file, operations such as:
•	retention
•	deletion
•	recovery
•	indexing
•	log management
would become difficult.
Instead:
Partition
   |
   +── Segment 0
   +── Segment 1
   +── Segment 2
   +── Segment 3
Now Kafka can delete an old segment:
Segment 0 → delete
without rewriting the entire partition.
This is a huge architectural simplification.
________________________________________
4. Segment naming
You'll often see files conceptually like:
00000000000000000000.log
00000000000000000000.index
00000000000000000000.timeindex
The number represents the base offset of the segment.
For example:
00000000000000000000.log
means:
base offset = 0
Another:
00000000000000100000.log
means:
base offset = 100000
So:
Partition P0

Segment A
base offset = 0

Segment B
base offset = 100000

Segment C
base offset = 200000
________________________________________
5. The .log file
The .log file contains the actual Kafka record batches.
Conceptually:
Segment

.log

┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ RecordBatch                  │
│ RecordBatch                  │
│ RecordBatch                  │
│ RecordBatch                  │
└─────────────────────────────┘
Modern Kafka stores records in batches, rather than treating every record as an isolated disk operation.
That's important for performance.
Instead of:
write record
write record
write record
write record
the producer can send a batch:
┌───────────────────────────────┐
│ Record 1                      │
│ Record 2                      │
│ Record 3                      │
│ Record 4                      │
└───────────────────────────────┘
Kafka appends the batch to the log.
________________________________________
6. Why batching matters
Imagine 1 million messages/sec.
If each message requires expensive individual processing:
record
 → syscall
 → disk/network
 → record
 → syscall
 → ...
overhead becomes significant.
Instead:
1000 records
     ↓
one batch
     ↓
network operation
     ↓
append
You amortize overhead across many records.
This is one of the recurring principles in distributed systems:
Batching reduces per-operation overhead.
You'll see this again when we study the producer.
________________________________________
7. Offset assignment
Suppose a partition currently ends at:
offset 99
A new batch contains 4 records.
Kafka assigns:
100
101
102
103
Conceptually:
Existing:

97
98
99

New batch:

100
101
102
103
Offsets are monotonically increasing within a partition.
There is no requirement that:
P0 offset 500
has anything to do with:
P1 offset 500
________________________________________
8. Important subtlety: offset is not a byte position
This is a common misunderstanding.
You might think:
offset 100
=
byte 100
No.
Offset:
100
means:
the logical record position in that partition.
The physical byte position could be something like:
byte 8,392,120
So Kafka needs a way to map:
logical offset
        ↓
physical file position
That's where the index comes in.
________________________________________
9. The .index file
Suppose:
Partition P0
has records:
offset 1000
offset 1001
offset 1002
...
offset 5000
Kafka doesn't necessarily need:
index[1000]
index[1001]
index[1002]
...
index[5000]
Instead, Kafka uses a sparse index.
Conceptually:
Offset       Physical position

1000   →     0
1100   →     12000
1200   →     24500
1300   →     36700
Not every offset needs an index entry.
________________________________________
10. Why sparse indexing works
Suppose consumer asks:
"Give me offset 1250."
Kafka can find the nearest indexed offset:
1200 → byte 24500
Then scan forward from there:
1200
1201
1202
...
1250
So instead of searching the entire partition:
10 million records
it can:
index lookup
     ↓
nearby byte position
     ↓
sequential scan
This is an elegant combination of:
fast lookup
+
sequential I/O
________________________________________
11. This is an important systems-design pattern
You will see the same idea elsewhere.
Instead of building a huge expensive index:
every item indexed
you can sometimes use:
sparse index
+
sequential scan
This trades a little CPU scanning for significantly lower index overhead.
Kafka's append-log architecture benefits heavily from this approach.
________________________________________
12. Time index
Kafka also maintains a time-based index.
Conceptually:
timestamp → offset
For example:
10:00 → offset 1000
10:05 → offset 2500
10:10 → offset 4000
This helps Kafka locate records based on timestamps.
For example:
"Start consuming records from approximately 10:05."
Kafka can locate an appropriate offset and then scan.
________________________________________
13. Finding a record
Let's trace:
Consumer asks:
offset = 12,345
Kafka roughly needs to do:
Partition
   |
   v
Find segment containing 12,345
   |
   v
Segment index
   |
   v
Find nearest indexed offset
   |
   v
Physical byte position
   |
   v
Sequential scan
   |
   v
offset 12,345
That's an important internal flow.
________________________________________
14. Segment selection
Suppose:
Segment A
base = 0

Segment B
base = 100,000

Segment C
base = 200,000
Consumer asks:
offset = 150,000
Kafka knows:
100,000 ≤ 150,000 < 200,000
Therefore:
Segment B
is the relevant segment.
Then it searches the segment's index.
________________________________________
15. Active segment
One segment is normally the active segment.
New records are appended there.
Conceptually:
P0

Segment 0    CLOSED
Segment 1    CLOSED
Segment 2    CLOSED
Segment 3    ACTIVE
New records:
append
  ↓
Segment 3
Once the segment reaches configured limits, Kafka rolls to a new segment:
Before:

Segment 3 ACTIVE

After:

Segment 3 CLOSED
Segment 4 ACTIVE
________________________________________
16. What causes segment rolling?
There are configuration-based limits related to:
•	segment size
•	segment time
Conceptually:
if segment too large
      ↓
roll

or

if segment too old
      ↓
roll
This matters enormously for retention.
Because Kafka generally operates on segments, not individual records, for physical deletion.
________________________________________
17. Retention
Suppose retention is:
7 days
and your partition contains:
Day 1
Day 2
Day 3
Day 4
Day 5
Day 6
Day 7
Day 8
Kafka can eventually delete old segments:
Day 1 segment → delete
Day 2 segment → delete
while keeping newer segments.
Conceptually:
Partition

oldest
  ↓
[Day 1] ❌
[Day 2] ❌
[Day 3] ✓
[Day 4] ✓
...
[Day 8] ✓
  ↑
newest
The exact deletion timing can be approximate because cleanup happens asynchronously.
________________________________________
18. Retention is not based on consumer progress
This is a very important point.
Suppose:
Consumer A
offset = 100
while Kafka has records:
0 ... 1,000,000
If retention says old data should be deleted, Kafka can delete it even though the consumer hasn't processed it.
So:
Kafka retention and consumer offsets are separate concepts.
If a consumer falls behind beyond the retained data, it can encounter an offset that is no longer available.
That's one reason consumer lag and retention planning matter.
________________________________________
19. Log compaction
Now we introduce another major Kafka concept:
Log compaction is different from time/size-based retention.
Suppose topic contains records keyed by:
user-123
Events:
user-123 → name=Alice
user-123 → name=Bob
user-123 → name=Charlie
With ordinary retention, all may remain until expiration.
With compaction, Kafka can eventually retain the latest value for a key:
user-123 → name=Charlie
Conceptually:
Before:

user-123 → Alice
user-123 → Bob
user-123 → Charlie

After compaction:

user-123 → Charlie
This is useful for topics representing current state.
________________________________________
20. Compaction does NOT mean "only latest record exists immediately"
This is a common misconception.
Compaction happens asynchronously.
For some period you can have:
Alice
Bob
Charlie
Eventually the log cleaner may compact older values.
Therefore compaction provides a policy, not an instantaneous map structure.
________________________________________
21. Tombstones
Suppose:
user-123 → Charlie
and you want to delete that key.
Kafka can represent deletion using a tombstone:
user-123 → null
Conceptually:
Alice
Bob
Charlie
TOMBSTONE
Compaction can eventually remove the old values and preserve the deletion semantics appropriately.
This is particularly useful for compacted state topics.
________________________________________
22. Retention vs compaction
Think:
Time/size retention
"How long / how much data do I keep?"
Compaction
"Which records for the same key do I need to retain?"
They solve different problems.
________________________________________
23. Partition is the unit of replication too
This is another critical mental model.
Kafka doesn't replicate an entire topic as one object.
It replicates individual partitions.
Suppose:
Topic: orders

P0
P1
P2
Replication happens per partition:
P0 → replicas
P1 → replicas
P2 → replicas
We'll explore this deeply in Lesson 7.
________________________________________
24. Partition placement
Suppose we have:
Broker 1
Broker 2
Broker 3
and:
orders
6 partitions
replication factor = 3
A simplified conceptual layout might be:
             Leader   Replica Replica

P0             B1       B2      B3
P1             B2       B3      B1
P2             B3       B1      B2
P3             B1       B3      B2
P4             B2       B1      B3
P5             B3       B2      B1
Notice the distribution.
This is how Kafka combines:
partitioning
+
replication
to achieve scalability and fault tolerance.
________________________________________
25. The partition is the bottleneck boundary
Suppose one partition can sustain approximately:
X MB/s
Your topic has:
1 partition
Then the topic's write throughput is fundamentally constrained by that partition.
Increase to:
10 partitions
and you can distribute work:
P0 → Broker 1
P1 → Broker 2
P2 → Broker 3
...
Potential throughput increases.
But don't interpret:
10 partitions = exactly 10× throughput
Real systems have other bottlenecks:
network
disk
CPU
broker limits
replication
producer batching
consumer processing
________________________________________
26. More partitions aren't always better
This is an important production lesson.
You might think:
"Just create 10,000 partitions and get massive parallelism."
Not so fast.
Each partition creates metadata and operational overhead.
More partitions mean more:
files
metadata
replica management
leader management
consumer assignments
recovery work
network connections
And when a broker fails:
100 partitions
is much easier to recover/manage than:
100,000 partitions
So partition count is an architectural capacity decision.
________________________________________
27. Partition count affects future scaling
Suppose you start with:
orders = 3 partitions
Later you need:
20 consumers
You can't get 20-way partition parallelism from only 3 partitions.
You can increase the partition count, but there's an important consequence:
Increasing partition count can change key-to-partition mapping and therefore affect ordering for keyed records.
For example, if partition selection depends on:
hash(key) % partitionCount
then changing:
3 → 6 partitions
can cause a key to map to a different partition.
Therefore:
Partition count should be planned carefully when ordering semantics depend on keys.
________________________________________
28. Design Exercise — Partition calculation
Imagine:
Traffic = 500 MB/s
You estimate one partition can safely handle:
50 MB/s
You need some safety margin.
Naively:
500 / 50 = 10 partitions
But production design shouldn't stop there.
Ask:
•	What's peak traffic?
•	Is 500 MB/s average or peak?
•	What's replication factor?
•	What's consumer throughput?
•	What's broker network capacity?
•	What's disk throughput?
•	What's acceptable lag?
•	How much headroom do you want?
Suppose peak is:
800 MB/s
and you want:
50% headroom
Now your calculation changes.
This is the type of capacity reasoning I want you to practice.
