Lesson 4 — Kafka Consumer Internals
We now move to the consumer side.
A useful mental model is:
A Kafka consumer does not receive messages pushed by Kafka. The consumer actively pulls data from the broker.
The architecture looks like:
Kafka Partition
      │
      │ FetchRequest
      ▼
Kafka Broker
      │
      │ records
      ▼
Consumer network I/O
      │
      ▼
Consumer buffer
      │
      ▼
poll()
      │
      ▼
Application
      │
      ▼
Business processing
Today we'll understand what happens inside that pipeline.
________________________________________
1. Why does Kafka use pull instead of push?
Compare two architectures.
Push model
Broker
  │
  │ "Here's another message"
  ▼
Consumer
The broker decides when to send.
Pull model
Consumer
  │
  │ "Give me records"
  ▼
Broker
The consumer controls consumption.
Kafka chose the second model.
Why?
Because consumers have different processing capabilities.
Imagine:
Consumer A → 50,000 msg/sec
Consumer B → 1,000 msg/sec
If the broker aggressively pushes data to both, Consumer B can become overwhelmed.
With pull:
Fast consumer
    ↓
fetch frequently

Slow consumer
    ↓
fetch less / process more slowly
The consumer has more control over its own rate.
This is fundamentally a backpressure-friendly design.
________________________________________
2. The consumer doesn't call Kafka for every record
This is another important misconception.
You might imagine:
poll()
 ↓
one record
Then:
poll()
 ↓
one record
That's inefficient.
Instead:
poll()
   ↓
many records
   ↓
process locally
The consumer fetches batches of records.
Conceptually:
Broker
   |
   | FetchRequest
   |
   v
┌──────────────────────┐
│ record 101            │
│ record 102            │
│ record 103            │
│ record 104            │
│ record 105            │
└──────────────────────┘
Then your application consumes them from memory.
________________________________________
3. Consumer internals
A simplified architecture:
                 Kafka Consumer
                       │
          ┌────────────┴────────────┐
          │                         │
          ▼                         ▼
   Application thread         Network I/O
          │                         │
          │ poll()                  │
          ▼                         │
     Consumer state                 │
          │                         │
          ▼                         │
      Fetch buffer ◄──────── FetchResponse
                                    │
                                    ▼
                                  Broker
The exact implementation has evolved across Kafka versions, but this conceptual separation is extremely useful.
________________________________________
4. What happens when you call poll()?
Suppose:
ConsumerRecords<String, Order> records =
        consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(1000));
Don't think:
poll()
 ↓
TCP request
 ↓
one message
 ↓
return
Instead, conceptually:
poll()
  ↓
check locally buffered records
  ↓
if enough data exists
     return records
  ↓
otherwise ensure fetches are happening
  ↓
wait for data / fetch response
  ↓
return a batch
So poll() is an important part of the consumer's coordination and fetching lifecycle.
________________________________________
5. Consumer continuously fetches
Imagine your consumer owns:
P0
P1
The consumer can issue fetch requests such as:
FetchRequest:

P0 → offset 1000
P1 → offset 2500
Broker responds:
FetchResponse:

P0:
1000
1001
1002
...

P1:
2500
2501
2502
...
The consumer stores fetched records locally.
________________________________________
6. Why prefetch?
Suppose processing one batch takes:
100 ms
If the consumer waited until processing finished before requesting the next data:
fetch
 ↓
process 100ms
 ↓
fetch
 ↓
process 100ms
network latency would add unnecessary idle time.
Instead:
Network
   ↓
prefetch
   ↓
buffer

Application
   ↓
process current batch
While your application is processing:
Batch A
the consumer can be preparing:
Batch B
Batch C
This is pipeline parallelism.
________________________________________
7. Consumer fetch buffer
Conceptually:
Broker
   |
   | records
   v
Consumer
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ P0: records             │
│ P0: records             │
│ P1: records             │
│ P1: records             │
└─────────────────────────┘
           |
           v
         poll()
           |
           v
      application
The buffer decouples:
network speed
from:
application processing speed
up to the limits imposed by memory/configuration.
________________________________________
8. fetch.min.bytes
Now we reach an important consumer setting.
fetch.min.bytes
Conceptually:
Don't return a fetch response immediately if very little data is available; wait until at least approximately this much data is available or the fetch wait limit expires.
Why?
Suppose your consumer makes:
1000 fetch requests
and each gets:
1 KB
That's lots of network overhead.
Instead:
fetch.min.bytes = larger value
can encourage larger responses.
Conceptually:
Without batching:

fetch → 1 KB
fetch → 1 KB
fetch → 1 KB
fetch → 1 KB

With larger fetch:

fetch → 64 KB
This can improve throughput.
________________________________________
9. But fetch.min.bytes has a trade-off
Larger minimum fetch sizes can mean:
higher throughput
but potentially:
higher latency
because Kafka may wait for more data.
So again:
throughput
     ↕
latency
This same trade-off appears throughout distributed systems.
________________________________________
10. fetch.max.wait.ms
What if the broker doesn't have enough data to satisfy:
fetch.min.bytes
Should it wait forever?
No.
There's:
fetch.max.wait.ms
Conceptually:
Fetch request
     |
     +--> enough data?
     |       |
     |      YES → respond
     |
     +--> NO
             |
             ↓
        wait up to limit
             |
             ↓
          respond
Therefore:
fetch.min.bytes
+
fetch.max.wait.ms
work together.
________________________________________
11. Example
Suppose:
fetch.min.bytes = 64 KB
fetch.max.wait.ms = 50 ms
At:
t = 0
consumer requests data.
At:
t = 20 ms
only:
20 KB
is available.
Kafka can continue waiting.
At:
t = 40 ms
data reaches:
64 KB
Response can be returned.
Alternatively, if only:
30 KB
arrives by the wait limit, Kafka can return what is available rather than waiting forever.
________________________________________
12. max.poll.records
Now there's another setting:
max.poll.records
This controls approximately how many records the consumer returns to your application in one poll().
For example:
max.poll.records = 500
means your application won't get an arbitrarily huge number of records from a single poll.
Important distinction:
fetch size
and:
poll records
are not the same thing.
The consumer may fetch a larger amount of data internally and then expose records to your application through successive poll() calls.
________________________________________
13. Three different batch boundaries
This is worth memorizing.
Kafka consumer has multiple layers:
Broker response
       ↓
fetch
       ↓
consumer buffer
       ↓
poll()
       ↓
application batch
Therefore:
fetch.min.bytes
is about fetching data from brokers.
While:
max.poll.records
is about how many records are returned to the application from a poll.
Don't treat them as synonyms.
________________________________________
14. Why consumers poll?
Now let's look at the Java API:
while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Order> records =
        consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(1000));

    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Order> record : records) {
        process(record);
    }
}
It looks simple.
But poll() participates in several things:
fetching
record delivery
consumer group coordination
liveness
partition assignment state
offset-related state
That's why Kafka consumers have several seemingly strange timeout configurations.
________________________________________
15. The famous max.poll.interval.ms
Suppose:
poll()
 ↓
500 records
 ↓
process records
 ↓
next poll()
What if processing takes:
10 minutes
while:
max.poll.interval.ms = 5 minutes
Then Kafka can conclude:
This consumer isn't making progress.
It may trigger a group rebalance.
This protects the consumer group from a member that has effectively stopped polling.
________________________________________
16. Why not just use session.timeout.ms?
Because these are solving different problems.
Session timeout
Primarily concerns whether the consumer remains alive/connected enough for group membership, with heartbeats involved.
Max poll interval
Concerns:
How long can the application go between successful poll() calls?
Think:
Heartbeat
    ↓
"Am I alive?"

poll interval
    ↓
"Am I making application progress?"
That's a simplified mental model, but a useful one.
________________________________________
17. Slow processing problem
Suppose:
poll()
 ↓
1000 records
 ↓
process each = 100 ms
Worst-case processing time:
1000 × 100 ms
=
100 seconds
But:
max.poll.interval.ms = 60 seconds
Then the consumer may be removed from the group before it gets back to polling.
This can cause:
rebalance
   ↓
partitions reassigned
   ↓
consumer processing problems
Therefore, consumer batch size and processing time must be designed together.
________________________________________
18. A useful capacity equation
If:
N = records returned per poll
T = worst-case processing time per record
then approximate processing time:
poll processing time ≈ N × T
You need that comfortably below:
max.poll.interval.ms
For example:
500 records
×
20 ms
=
10 seconds
If:
max.poll.interval = 5 minutes
you're comfortable.
But:
5000 records
×
100 ms
=
500 seconds
would be dangerous with a 5-minute interval.
This is exactly the sort of reasoning you should use in production design.
________________________________________
19. What if one message takes 30 seconds?
Suppose:
max.poll.interval = 5 minutes
and one record occasionally takes:
6 minutes
Your consumer may not call poll() within the allowed interval.
That creates a problem.
Possible architectural solutions include:
smaller poll batches
parallel processing
pause/resume partitions
move long-running work elsewhere
increase max.poll.interval
redesign processing
But each has consequences.
________________________________________
20. Consumer processing architecture
For ordinary workloads:
poll
 ↓
process
 ↓
poll
 ↓
process
For expensive work, you might architect:
                ┌→ Worker 1
                |
poll → queue ───┼→ Worker 2
                |
                └→ Worker 3
But now you have to carefully manage:
offset commits
ordering
backpressure
partition ownership
duplicate processing
shutdown
This is where "just add threads" becomes dangerous.
________________________________________
21. Ordering and consumer threads
Suppose partition:
P0:
100
101
102
103
You create four workers:
Worker 1 → 100
Worker 2 → 101
Worker 3 → 102
Worker 4 → 103
Now:
102
could finish before:
100
You've destroyed processing order.
Kafka guarantees ordering when consuming records sequentially from a partition, but your application can destroy that ordering after poll().
This is a crucial distinction.
Kafka's ordering guarantee does not automatically make your business processing ordered.
________________________________________
22. Partition-level parallelism
The safer parallelism model is:
P0 → Worker/Consumer A
P1 → Worker/Consumer B
P2 → Worker/Consumer C
Then:
P0:
100 → 101 → 102

P1:
200 → 201 → 202
can process concurrently while each partition remains ordered.
This is one reason partitions are such an important Kafka abstraction.
________________________________________
23. Consumer heartbeats
Now let's introduce group membership.
Kafka consumers send heartbeats to the group coordinator.
Conceptually:
Consumer
   |
   | heartbeat
   v
Coordinator
The heartbeat communicates:
"I'm still an active member of this consumer group."
If heartbeats stop for too long, the coordinator can consider the consumer dead and trigger partition reassignment.
________________________________________
24. Why separate heartbeat from poll()?
Modern Kafka consumer implementations can maintain heartbeats separately from application polling.
This allows:
Application processing
        +
heartbeat activity
to be decoupled to some extent.
But that does not mean you can stop calling poll() forever.
max.poll.interval.ms still matters.
So don't think:
"Heartbeats mean I can process forever without polling."
No.
You have two different liveness dimensions.
________________________________________
25. Three important time concepts
Keep these separate:
session.timeout.ms
→ consumer/group liveness detection.
heartbeat.interval.ms
→ how frequently heartbeats are sent.
max.poll.interval.ms
→ maximum allowed interval between application polls before the consumer is considered to be failing to make progress.
A simplified picture:
Consumer
   |
   | heartbeat heartbeat heartbeat
   |     |        |        |
   v     v        v        v
Coordinator

Application:
poll()
  ↓
processing
  ↓
poll()
  ↓
processing
________________________________________
26. What happens when a consumer crashes?
Suppose:
Group:

Consumer A → P0
Consumer B → P1
Consumer C → P2
Consumer B crashes.
Eventually the group coordinator detects that B is gone.
Then:
P1
 ↓
unassigned
 ↓
rebalance
 ↓
assigned to A or C
For example:
Before:

A → P0
B → P1
C → P2

After:

A → P0,P1
C → P2
We'll study this in depth in Consumer Groups.
________________________________________
27. Offset position vs committed offset
This is critical.
Suppose consumer has fetched:
100
101
102
103
104
and processed:
100
101
There are multiple notions of "where the consumer is."
Conceptually:
Fetched position
        ↓
104

Processed position
        ↓
101

Committed offset
        ↓
maybe 101
These are not necessarily identical.
This distinction becomes extremely important when we study offset management.
________________________________________
28. Example of duplicate processing
Suppose:
Kafka:
100
101
102
Consumer processes:
100 ✓
101 ✓
Then crashes before committing the corresponding offset.
After restart:
consumer resumes
       ↓
101
So:
101
may be processed again.
This is why Kafka consumers commonly operate with at-least-once processing semantics unless you build stronger guarantees.
We'll dedicate the next lesson to offsets.
________________________________________
29. Why poll() does not mean "message consumed forever"
This is a subtle but extremely important conceptual point.
When:
consumer.poll(...)
returns a record, that does not mean:
Kafka has permanently marked this message as processed.
It means:
The consumer has fetched and exposed the record to your application.
Durable progress depends on offset management.
So:
poll()
and:
commit
are separate concepts.
________________________________________
30. Consumer failure timeline
Let's trace a realistic failure.
Kafka
  |
  | 100,101,102
  v
Consumer
  |
  | poll()
  v
Application
  |
  | process 100 ✓
  | process 101 ✓
  | process 102 💥
  |
  X crash
Suppose committed offset was still:
100
After restart:
consumer
   |
   ↓
resume from committed position
   |
   ↓
101
So 101 may be processed again.
This is why offset commit placement matters.
________________________________________
31. Consumer throughput
At a high level:
Consumer throughput
≈
fetch efficiency
×
network capacity
×
number of partitions
×
application processing capacity
If Kafka can provide:
500 MB/s
but your application can process:
50 MB/s
Kafka isn't your bottleneck.
Your application is.
Conversely:
application can process 500 MB/s
Kafka delivers 50 MB/s
then fetching/network/partition distribution becomes relevant.
________________________________________
32. Consumer lag
Suppose Kafka has:
latest offset = 1,000,000
and consumer has processed approximately:
900,000
Then lag is roughly:
100,000 records
Conceptually:
Kafka head
     |
     v
1,000,000
     |
     | 100k lag
     |
     v
900,000
     |
     v
Consumer progress
Lag is one of the most important operational metrics for Kafka consumers.
But remember:
Lag is measured in records/offsets, not directly in seconds.
A lag of 100,000 could mean:
2 seconds of traffic
or:
2 hours of traffic
depending on workload.
________________________________________
33. A better operational metric: lag + throughput
Suppose:
lag = 1,000,000 records
consumer processing = 100,000 records/sec
Roughly:
recovery time ≈ 10 sec
But if producer traffic is also:
80,000 records/sec
then the consumer's net catch-up rate is only:
100,000 - 80,000
=
20,000 records/sec
So:
1,000,000 / 20,000
≈ 50 seconds
This is much more useful operational reasoning.
