Lesson 5 — Kafka Consumer Groups
This is one of the most important Kafka lessons because consumer groups are where Kafka turns partitions into a distributed parallel-processing system.
The core idea is surprisingly simple:
Within one consumer group, each partition is assigned to at most one consumer at a time.
Everything else—scaling, rebalancing, failover, lag distribution—builds around this rule.
________________________________________
1. Start with the simplest model
Suppose:
Topic: orders

P0
P1
P2
P3
One consumer:
C1
Then:
C1 → P0
   → P1
   → P2
   → P3
One consumer can consume multiple partitions.
Now add another consumer:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2 P3
Kafka distributes partitions across consumers.
Add another:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2
C3 → P3
The important observation:
4 partitions
3 consumers
means maximum useful parallelism is 4, not 3.
________________________________________
2. The fundamental rule
Within a consumer group:
Partition → one consumer
at a given point in time.
For example:
Group A

P0 → C1
P1 → C1
P2 → C2
P3 → C3
But you cannot have:
P0 → C1
P0 → C2
simultaneously within the same group.
Why?
Because then both consumers could independently process:
P0 offset 100
P0 offset 101
P0 offset 102
and the group would no longer represent a coordinated consumption position.
________________________________________
3. Multiple groups are different
Now consider:
Topic: orders

P0 P1 P2
Group A:
C1 → P0
C2 → P1
C3 → P2
Group B:
C4 → P0
C5 → P1
C6 → P2
This is perfectly valid.
Why?
Because groups represent independent consumers of the topic.
For example:
orders
   |
   +------→ Order Processing Group
   |
   +------→ Analytics Group
   |
   +------→ Fraud Detection Group
Each group maintains its own consumption progress.
________________________________________
4. Kafka is not a traditional queue
This distinction is extremely important.
Traditional queue:
Message
   ↓
Consumer
   ↓
message removed
Kafka:
Partition
-------------------------
100
101
102
103
104
-------------------------
          ↑
        offset
Consumers track where they are.
The data remains in Kafka according to retention policies even after consumers process it.
So:
Group A → offset 1000
Group B → offset 500
Group C → offset 200
can all consume the same underlying records independently.
This is one of Kafka's most powerful architectural properties.
________________________________________
5. Consumer group as a logical application
Think of:
group.id = order-service
as meaning:
"All instances belonging to this group collectively form one logical consumer application."
Suppose you deploy:
order-service instance 1
order-service instance 2
order-service instance 3
All use:
group.id=order-service
Kafka sees:
Consumer Group
       |
       +--- C1
       +--- C2
       +--- C3
They cooperate.
________________________________________
6. Scaling a consumer group
Suppose:
12 partitions
and:
1 consumer
Then:
C1 → P0 P1 P2 P3 P4 P5 P6 P7 P8 P9 P10 P11
Processing parallelism is limited.
Add consumers:
3 consumers
approximately:
C1 → P0 P1 P2 P3
C2 → P4 P5 P6 P7
C3 → P8 P9 P10 P11
Now work can be processed concurrently.
________________________________________
7. What if we add too many consumers?
Suppose:
3 partitions
10 consumers
You cannot get 10-way partition-level parallelism.
For example:
C1 → P0
C2 → P1
C3 → P2

C4 → idle
C5 → idle
C6 → idle
...
So:
useful consumers ≤ partitions
This is a fundamental Kafka scaling constraint.
________________________________________
8. Design consequence
Suppose you expect:
20 consumer instances
and want all of them doing useful Kafka work.
You need enough partitions.
For example:
20 consumers
20 partitions
or more.
But don't conclude:
"Just create 10,000 partitions."
Partition count has costs.
More partitions means more:
•	metadata
•	files/segments
•	replication work
•	leader management
•	consumer coordination
•	network connections
•	recovery work
Partition count is an architectural decision.
________________________________________
9. Who decides partition assignment?
This is where the group coordinator enters.
Kafka needs some broker responsible for coordinating a consumer group.
Conceptually:
Consumer Group
      |
      v
Group Coordinator
      |
      +---- C1
      +---- C2
      +---- C3
The coordinator manages group membership and coordinates rebalancing.
________________________________________
10. How is the coordinator selected?
Kafka determines a group coordinator based on the consumer group's identity.
Conceptually, the group ID is mapped to a particular internal group-coordination partition, and the broker leading that partition becomes the coordinator.
You don't need to memorize the hashing details yet.
The important mental model is:
group.id
   ↓
group coordination metadata
   ↓
coordinator broker
So different groups may have different coordinators.
________________________________________
11. Consumer joins the group
Suppose:
Topic:
P0 P1 P2 P3
and C1 starts.
It joins:
group = order-service
Conceptually:
C1
 |
 | JoinGroup
 v
Coordinator
The coordinator now knows:
Members:
C1
The group needs an assignment.
________________________________________
12. The group leader
One consumer is selected as the group leader for assignment purposes.
This is easy to misunderstand.
The group leader is not Kafka's partition leader.
These are completely different concepts.
Partition leader
Responsible for a Kafka partition's replication/write/read leadership.
Consumer group leader
A consumer selected to help calculate the consumer group's partition assignment.
Think:
Kafka partition leader
    ↓
storage/replication responsibility
versus:
consumer group leader
    ↓
partition assignment responsibility
Do not mix them.
________________________________________
13. Assignment process
Conceptually:
Consumers
   |
   | JoinGroup
   v
Coordinator
   |
   | selects group leader
   v
Group Leader
   |
   | calculates assignment
   v
Coordinator
   |
   | sends assignments
   v
Consumers
For example:
P0 P1 P2 P3

C1
C2
Assignment might become:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2 P3
________________________________________
14. Rebalancing
Now suppose C3 joins.
Before:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2 P3
After C3 joins, Kafka needs to redistribute partitions.
Potentially:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2
C3 → P3
This redistribution process is called:
Rebalancing
This is one of the most important Kafka concepts.
________________________________________
15. Why rebalancing exists
Suppose C2 owns:
P2 P3
and crashes.
If Kafka did nothing:
P2 P3
   ↓
nobody consuming
That would be bad.
So Kafka needs:
detect failure
     ↓
remove consumer
     ↓
reassign partitions
     ↓
resume processing
That's rebalancing.
________________________________________
16. Failure example
Before failure:
Group

C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2 P3
C3 → P4 P5
C2 crashes:
C1 → P0 P1
C2 → 💥
C3 → P4 P5
Now:
P2 P3
need new owners.
Possible assignment:
C1 → P0 P1 P2
C3 → P3 P4 P5
The exact assignment depends on the assignor.
