21. Assignment strategies
Kafka needs an algorithm to answer:
Which consumer gets which partitions?
This is called a partition assignor.
Several strategies exist.
We'll focus conceptually on:
•	Range
•	RoundRobin
•	Sticky
•	Cooperative Sticky
________________________________________
22. Range assignment
Suppose:
Partitions:
P0 P1 P2 P3 P4 P5

Consumers:
C1 C2
A range-style assignment might produce:
C1 → P0 P1 P2
C2 → P3 P4 P5
Simple.
But with multiple topics, range assignment can create uneven distributions.
________________________________________
23. RoundRobin
Conceptually:
P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C1
P3 → C2
P4 → C1
P5 → C2
Result:
C1 → P0 P2 P4
C2 → P1 P3 P5
More evenly distributed in many scenarios.
But it may move more partitions during changes.
________________________________________
24. Sticky assignment
Now we introduce an important production concern.
Suppose:
Before:

C1 → P0 P1
C2 → P2 P3
C3 joins.
A naive rebalance might produce:
C1 → P0
C2 → P2
C3 → P1 P3
Lots of movement.
Sticky assignment tries to preserve existing ownership where possible:
C1 → P0
C2 → P2
C3 → P1 P3
while minimizing unnecessary movement, subject to balance.
Why is minimizing movement useful?
Because partition movement means:
consumer state changes
cache warming
processing interruption
potential latency
________________________________________
25. Cooperative rebalancing
Traditional rebalancing can be relatively disruptive.
Cooperative rebalancing tries to make ownership changes more incremental.
Conceptually:
Eager style
Everyone gives up partitions
        ↓
reassign everything
        ↓
resume
Cooperative style
Only necessary partitions move
        ↓
incremental transition
        ↓
continue processing where possible
This can significantly reduce disruption.
You don't need to memorize the protocol details yet.
The key architectural idea is:
Don't stop the entire world just because ownership of a few partitions needs to change.
________________________________________
26. Static membership
Another useful Kafka concept is static membership.
Imagine Kubernetes:
consumer-0
consumer-1
consumer-2
A pod temporarily restarts.
Without static membership, Kafka may interpret:
consumer disappeared
and trigger rebalancing.
Then:
consumer returns
and another rebalance happens.
Static membership can reduce unnecessary rebalances when consumers temporarily disconnect/restart while retaining their logical identities.
Conceptually:
temporary failure
      ↓
don't immediately treat as permanent group membership change
This is particularly useful in some containerized deployments.
________________________________________
27. Consumer group as distributed ownership
You can now think of the group as:
                 Consumer Group

P0 ───────────────→ C1
P1 ───────────────→ C1
P2 ───────────────→ C2
P3 ───────────────→ C2
P4 ───────────────→ C3
The group maintains a distributed ownership mapping:
partition → consumer
When membership changes:
old mapping
     ↓
rebalance
     ↓
new mapping
This is essentially a distributed coordination problem.
________________________________________
28. Consumer group state machine
Conceptually, a group moves through states such as:
Empty
  ↓
Preparing to join
  ↓
Completing rebalance
  ↓
Stable
