49. The Kafka questions you should now be able to answer
As a 6+ year Java developer moving toward university-level system design, you should now be able to reason through questions like:
•	Why is Kafka faster than a traditional request/response architecture for event workloads?
•	Why are partitions necessary?
•	Why can't one partition be consumed by 10 consumers simultaneously within the same group?
•	Why does partition-key selection matter?
•	What creates a hot partition?
•	What happens when a broker dies?
•	Why is ISR important?
•	Why can acks=all still fail?
•	What does min.insync.replicas protect?
•	What happens when the leader dies before replication completes?
•	What is an unclean leader election?
•	Why does Kafka need leader epochs?
•	Why do producer retries create duplicates?
•	How does idempotent producer behavior help?
•	What problem do Kafka transactions solve?
•	Why doesn't Kafka EOS make PostgreSQL exactly-once?
•	Why do we need an outbox?
•	What is consumer lag?
•	Why can increasing consumers stop helping?
